arXiv:2510.02208v1 [eess.IV] 02 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Measurement-Guided Consistency Model Sampling for Inverse Problems</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Amirreza Tanevardi
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Pooria Abbas Rad Moghadam
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sajjad Amini
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Amirreza Tanevardi (e-mail: amirreza.tanverdi@ee.sharif.edu) and Pooria Abbas Rad Moghadam (e-mail: p.abbasrad@ee.sharif.edu) are with the Department of Electrical Engineering, Sharif University of Technology, Tehran, Iran. 
<br class="ltx_break">Sajjad Amini (e-mail: s_amini@sharif.edu) is with the Electronics Research Institute, Sharif University of Technology, Tehran, Iran.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models have become powerful generative priors for solving inverse imaging problems, but their reliance on slow multi-step sampling limits practical deployment. Consistency models address this bottleneck by enabling high-quality generation in a single or only a few steps, yet their direct adaptation to inverse problems is underexplored. In this paper, we present a modified consistency sampling approach tailored for inverse problem reconstruction: the sampler’s stochasticity is guided by a measurement-consistency mechanism tied to the measurement operator, which enforces fidelity to the acquired measurements while retaining the efficiency of consistency-based generation. Experiments on Fashion-MNIST and LSUN Bedroom datasets demonstrate consistent improvements in perceptual and pixel-level metrics, including Fréchet Inception Distance, Kernel Inception Distance, peak signal-to-noise ratio, and structural similarity index measure, compared to baseline consistency sampling, yielding competitive or superior reconstructions with only a handful of steps.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>consistency models, diffusion models, inverse problems, measurement-consistency sampling

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inverse problems play a central role in imaging science and engineering, with applications ranging from medical imaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and audio signal processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> to remote sensing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and seismic imaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. The task is to recover an unknown signal <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from degraded measurements <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained via a degradation operator <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}=A(\boldsymbol{x})+n,\qquad n\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2}I)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}=A(\boldsymbol{x})+n,\qquad n\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2}I).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is often ill-conditioned, undersampled, or noisy, reconstruction is ill-posed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and hinges on strong priors. In a Bayesian view, the posterior can be calculated as:</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{y})\propto p(\boldsymbol{y}\mid\boldsymbol{x})\,p(\boldsymbol{x})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{y})\propto p(\boldsymbol{y}\mid\boldsymbol{x})\,p(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, the maximum a posteriori (MAP) solution can be obtained by maximizing the log-posterior probability as:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{\mathrm{MAP}}=\mathop{\mathrm{argmax}}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\{\log p(\boldsymbol{y}\mid\boldsymbol{x})+\log p(\boldsymbol{x})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>MAP</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">argmax</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}_{\mathrm{MAP}}=\mathop{\mathrm{argmax}}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\{\log p(\boldsymbol{y}\mid\boldsymbol{x})+\log p(\boldsymbol{x})\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where the log-likelihood term <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(\boldsymbol{y}\mid\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(\boldsymbol{y}\mid\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math> captures the <em id="S1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">measurement fidelity</em>, ensuring that the estimated solution <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is consistent with the observed measurements <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The log-prior term <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math> incorporates prior knowledge about the random vector <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as smoothness, sparsity, or structural constraints through the probability distribution function, and plays the role of <em id="S1.p1.4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regularization</em> that stabilizes the solution when the problem is ill-posed or the measurements are noisy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> are a class of generative models that learn to approximate the data distribution by reversing a gradual noising process. In the forward process, Gaussian noise is incrementally added to a clean data sample <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps, producing latent variables <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> according to:</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\mid\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t-1})=\mathcal{N}\!\left(\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\,\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1},\,\beta_{t}\mathbf{I}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\mid\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t-1})=\mathcal{N}\!\left(\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\,\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1},\,\beta_{t}\mathbf{I}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a variance schedule. The generative process reverse this diffusion by learning a parameterized distribution as:</p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}\!\left(\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t),\,\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t-1}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}\!\left(\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t),\,\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the covariance <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed according to the noise schedule, and the model only learns the mean <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Training is typically carried out by minimizing a simplified noise-prediction objective, where the model learns to estimate the noise added at each step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denoising Diffusion Restoration Models (DDRM) have recently emerged as powerful generative methods for posterior sampling in image inverse problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Building on the diffusion process, DDRM defines an iterative procedure to sample from the posterior of an inverse problem, using the diffusion model to capture the prior distribution. By modeling a forward noising process together with its reverse denoising dynamics, these priors enable posterior sampling strategies that produce high-quality reconstructions in a variety of applications, including super-resolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, inpainting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, and deblurring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
More precisely, the posterior sampling in DDRM is done using the following iterations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S1.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S1.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\theta}^{(t)}\!\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{t}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1},\boldsymbol{y}\right)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\theta}^{(t)}\!\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{t}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t+1},\boldsymbol{y}\right)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}\mathcal{N}\!\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}+\sqrt{1-\eta^{2}}\,\sigma_{t}\dfrac{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{t+1}-\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}}{\sigma_{t+1}},\,\eta^{2}\sigma_{t}^{2}\right),&amp;\!\!\!\text{if }s_{i}=0,\\[7.0pt]
\mathcal{N}\!\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}+\sqrt{1-\eta^{2}}\,\sigma_{t}\dfrac{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{t+1}-\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}}{\sigma_{y}/s_{i}},\,\eta^{2}\sigma_{t}^{2}\right),&amp;\!\!\!\text{if }\sigma_{t}&lt;\tfrac{\sigma_{y}}{s_{i}},\\[7.0pt]
\mathcal{N}\!\left((1-\eta_{b})\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}+\eta_{b}\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)},\,\sigma_{t}^{2}-\tfrac{\sigma_{y}^{2}}{s_{i}^{2}}\eta_{b}^{2}\right),&amp;\!\!\!\text{if }\sigma_{t}\geq\tfrac{\sigma_{y}}{s_{i}},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}\mathcal{N}\!\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}+\sqrt{1-\eta^{2}}\,\sigma_{t}\dfrac{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{t+1}-\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}}{\sigma_{t+1}},\,\eta^{2}\sigma_{t}^{2}\right),&amp;\!\!\!\text{if }s_{i}=0,\\[7.0pt]
\mathcal{N}\!\left(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}+\sqrt{1-\eta^{2}}\,\sigma_{t}\dfrac{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{t+1}-\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}}{\sigma_{y}/s_{i}},\,\eta^{2}\sigma_{t}^{2}\right),&amp;\!\!\!\text{if }\sigma_{t}&lt;\tfrac{\sigma_{y}}{s_{i}},\\[7.0pt]
\mathcal{N}\!\left((1-\eta_{b})\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}+\eta_{b}\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)},\,\sigma_{t}^{2}-\tfrac{\sigma_{y}^{2}}{s_{i}^{2}}\eta_{b}^{2}\right),&amp;\!\!\!\text{if }\sigma_{t}\geq\tfrac{\sigma_{y}}{s_{i}},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the singular values of the measurement operator <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{y}}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{y}}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th index of the measurements in the spectral domain. We also define <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{(i)}_{\theta,t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th index of <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{\theta,t}=\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{x}_{\theta,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{\theta,t}=\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{x}_{\theta,t}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{\theta,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{\theta,t}</annotation></semantics></math>is the model’s prediction of the clean component at step <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The parameters <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> control the diffusion noise and measurement noise, respectively, while <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> adjust the stochasticity of sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A major limitation, however, is that sampling from diffusion models typically requires hundreds of iterative steps, which restricts their applicability in time-sensitive or large-scale settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Consistency models (CMs) were proposed to address this computational bottleneck by learning a direct mapping <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t)\approx\boldsymbol{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t)\approx\boldsymbol{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> that projects a noisy latent <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to a clean sample in one or a few evaluations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Training enforces self-consistency across noise levels and a boundary constraint at small noise, i.e.:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t)=f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime})\quad\forall\,t,t^{\prime}\in[\epsilon,T],\quad f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{\epsilon},\epsilon)=\boldsymbol{x}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t)=f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime})\quad\forall\,t,t^{\prime}\in[\epsilon,T],\quad f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{\epsilon},\epsilon)=\boldsymbol{x}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">which together ensure consistent predictions across noise scales and exact reconstruction at the lowest noise level. These constraints enable single-step or multi-step generation with dramatically reduced neural function evaluations (NFEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. These attractive speed–quality tradeoffs make CMs promising candidates as fast priors for inverse problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Directly applying CMs to inverse problems is nontrivial. Recent works (e.g., CoSIGN) mitigate this by introducing measurement conditioning via auxiliary encoders or by enforcing hard projections to satisfy <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A(\boldsymbol{x})-\boldsymbol{y}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A(\boldsymbol{x})-\boldsymbol{y}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> constraints, yielding few-step reconstructions competitive with slow diffusion solvers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Nevertheless, designing simple, principled samplers that couple CM sampling dynamics with measurement fidelity while preserving single/few-step efficiency remains an open problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose a novel deterministic sampling strategy that bridges this gap by coupling CM updates with an explicit measurement-driven guidance mechanism. Intuitively, the sampler adapts its stochasticity according to the measurement residual, injecting exploration when reconstructions deviate from the observations and producing stable deterministic updates as measurement fidelity improves. We demonstrate through experiments on Fashion-MNIST and LSUN Bedroom that this sampling method improves perceptual (FID, KID) and distortion (PSNR, SSIM) metrics over baseline CM sampling, achieving competitive reconstructions with only a handful of steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Method</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sampling from consistency models (CMs) is typically carried out through iterative denoising steps, where additional noise is injected to improve reconstruction quality and sample diversity. In the context of inverse problems, however, existing CM-based approaches generally underutilize the sampling process, relying instead on hard measurement constraints after sample generation to encourage reconstruction fidelity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denoising Diffusion Implicit Model (DDIM) sampling process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is a method used for diffusion models sampling, where a prediction of the clean sample is used to denoise the current intermediate sample to the next level. The same approach can be used with CMs, where instead of injecting noise into the sample each time, we use the DDIM step to achieve the next noisy latent for the few-step generative process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Adjusted DDIM (aDDIM) is an extension of DDIM that adapts the number and size of diffusion steps during sampling to accelerate generation while maintaining high-quality samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">. Motivated by the adjusted DDIM (aDDIM) sampler, we propose a measurement-aware sampler that adaptively adjusts its estimation of needed stochasticity according to the inverse problem residue. Our method reformulates the variance term present in the aDDIM formulation to incorporate direct measurement consistency, enabling application to arbitrary CMs trained for inverse tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">aDDIM Sampler</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The variance-exploding update in aDDIM can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}=f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}=f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}=\boldsymbol{x}_{t}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}=\boldsymbol{x}_{t}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{x}_{s}=\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}+\sqrt{\Bigl(\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}\Bigl)+\Bigl(\frac{1-\sqrt{\tfrac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}}{||\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}||}\Bigl)^{2}\boldsymbol{x}_{var,t}}\quad\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0em">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0em">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{x}_{s}=\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}+\sqrt{\Bigl(\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}\Bigl)+\Bigl(\frac{1-\sqrt{\tfrac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}}{||\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}||}\Bigl)^{2}\boldsymbol{x}_{var,t}}\quad\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=t_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the next noise level, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the current level, and
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{\mathrm{var},t}=\eta\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{\mathrm{var},t}=\eta\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> a tunable hyperparameter and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> the teacher signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The effectiveness of this modification can be understood by comparing the squared norms of DDIM iterates with those of the hypothetical correct samples. Ideally, one would draw a clean sample
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{*}\sim p(\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{*}\sim p(\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math>
and map it deterministically to the next latent:</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}=\Bigl(1-\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\Bigr)\boldsymbol{x}^{*}+\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}=\Bigl(1-\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\Bigr)\boldsymbol{x}^{*}+\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> carries the correct conditional variance, the resulting <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the proper distribution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S2.F1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.F1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2510.02208v1/new-note-Fig.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/275;" width="429" height="275" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Illustration of the proposed Inverse-aDDIM framework, where measurement consistency is enforced using the measurement residue</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">DDIM, however, instead of sampling
<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> from the true conditional distribution, replaces <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with model prediction, which is its conditional mean
<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}=\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}=\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>,
yielding the deterministic update:</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{\mathrm{DDIM}}=\Bigl(1-\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\Bigr)\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}+\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\boldsymbol{x}_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>DDIM</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{\mathrm{DDIM}}=\Bigl(1-\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\Bigr)\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}+\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\boldsymbol{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The conditional expectation of the difference of their squared norms thus becomes:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}\|^{2}-\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{DDIM}\|^{2}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]=\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}\|^{2}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]-\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{DDIM}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}\|^{2}-\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{DDIM}\|^{2}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]=\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}\|^{2}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]-\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{DDIM}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">And since <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{DDIM}=\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{DDIM}=\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math> :</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}\|^{2}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]-\|\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]\|^{2}=\mathrm{trace}\!\left(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.097em;" width="2.097em"><mi>trace</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}\|^{2}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]-\|\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}^{*}|\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]\|^{2}=\mathrm{trace}\!\left(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">The term <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{trace}\!\left(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.097em;" width="2.097em"><mi>trace</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{trace}\!\left(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]\right)</annotation></semantics></math> can be calculated as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{trace}\!\left(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]\right)=\Bigl(1-\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\Bigl)^{2}\mathrm{trace}(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.097em;" width="2.097em"><mi>trace</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Var</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0em">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>trace</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Var</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{trace}\!\left(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]\right)=\Bigl(1-\sqrt{\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}\Bigl)^{2}\mathrm{trace}(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{tance}(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tance</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{tance}(\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}])</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta||\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta||\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in aDDIM. <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{x}_{s}\mid\boldsymbol{x}_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the variance
that has been “dropped” by DDIM. Thus, DDIM produces iterates of systematically smaller norm, often manifesting as over-smoothed samples. The adjustment of the DDIM compensates for this by increasing the noise estimate that must be added to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This increase is proportional to the current conditional variance of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Reference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> uses this sampling algorithm within their training paradigm, where it requires a teacher signal <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be either a training-set data point (in case of consistency training) or the output of a teacher model (in case of consistency distillation). This requirement makes converting aDDIM to a form suitable for any CM inverse problem solver non-trivial.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adapting to Inverse Problems</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To extend the aDDIM framework to inverse problems, we propose replacing the variance term <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{\mathrm{var},t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{\mathrm{var},t}</annotation></semantics></math> with the <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inverse residue</em> defined as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which directly reflects fidelity to the measurement <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The above replacement yields a measurement-aware sampler that preserves the variance-compensating effect of aDDIM while removing the need for a teacher signal. The full sampling step can be formalized as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}=f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{y},t),~~\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}=\boldsymbol{x}_{t}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}=f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{y},t),~~\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}=\boldsymbol{x}_{t}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{x}_{s}=\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}+\sqrt{\Bigl(\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}\Bigl)+\Bigl(\frac{1-\sqrt{\tfrac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}}{||\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}||}\Bigl)^{2}\gamma\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}}\hat{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0em">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0em">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{x}_{s}=\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}+\sqrt{\Bigl(\frac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}\Bigl)+\Bigl(\frac{1-\sqrt{\tfrac{s^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}{t^{2}-t_{min}^{2}}}}{||\hat{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}||}\Bigl)^{2}\gamma\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}}\hat{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that the final sample will be the direct output of the CM <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{y},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t},\boldsymbol{y},t)</annotation></semantics></math> after a few iterations of the update.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To justify this substitution, consider a linear forward operator <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Expanding the expectation of the residue gives:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[\|A(\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})+n\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[\|A(\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})+n\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|A(\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})\|^{2}+\mathrm{trace}(\Sigma_{n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>trace</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|A(\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})\|^{2}+\mathrm{trace}(\Sigma_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> denotes measurement noise with covariance <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assuming Gaussian noise, we obtain:</p>
<table id="S2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}]=\|A(\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})\|^{2}+m\sigma_{y}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}]=\|A(\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})\|^{2}+m\sigma_{y}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the measurement dimension. Bounding the first term with eigenvalues of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> yields:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\lambda_{max}(A^{T}A)\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}+m\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\lambda_{max}(A^{T}A)\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}+m\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|A\|_{2}^{2}\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}+m\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|A\|_{2}^{2}\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}+m\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectral norm of the measurement model. The constant offset <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be ignored in practice, while <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be absorbed into the hyperparameter. Giving:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{y}-A\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\eta^{\prime}\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\eta^{\prime}\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the expected inverse residue remains less than <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\boldsymbol{x}-\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring that our proposed substitution preserves the variance-compensating property of aDDIM while not overshooting the correct variance trace estimate. This modification effectively adapts the algorithm to the measurement-consistency setting of inverse problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The general CM sampling process using the Inverse-aDDIM algorithm is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ II-A aDDIM Sampler ‣ II Method ‣ Measurement-Guided Consistency Model Sampling for Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where the new noisy latent obtained through our proposed algorithm is denoised via the Inverse CM solver in a few-step iterative manner.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the effectiveness of the proposed algorithm, we conducted experiments using the CoSIGN base model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> on the LSUN Bedroom dataset at a resolution of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. In this framework, a trainable ControlNet is employed to impose measurement constraints and guide the generation process, while keeping the CM backbone frozen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. In our study, we did not retrain any of the available checkpoints; instead, we trained ControlNet for the linear deblurring task, which was not previously included.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We further evaluated our approach on the Fashion-MNIST dataset at a resolution of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28\times 28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28\times 28</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. For this dataset, we adopted the base model from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, where the auxiliary network does not directly enforce the measurement by guiding the output generation but instead produces a representation of it, which is then provided to the CM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation covers three distinct inverse problems. The measurements were generated as follows: (i) for super-resolution, LSUN bedroom images were downsampled to one-quarter of their original resolution in both dimensions, while Fashion MNIST images were downsampled to half ; (ii) for inpainting, a central square region with half the side length of the image was masked; and (iii) for the deblurring task, a Gaussian blur kernel with a standard deviation of 5 for the LSUN bedroom dataset and 3 for the Fashion MNIST dataset was applied. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, Gaussian noise with standard deviation <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> was added to all measurements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We also evaluated our method on a non-linear deblurring task for the LSUN Bedroom dataset, where images were blurred using a learned kernel-based nonlinear model, despite the absence of a theoretical foundation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The baseline used for comparison is the standard few-step consistency model sampling introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. In the case of the CoSIGN model, this corresponds to sampling with the soft measurement constraint alone. Across all experiments, we employed 2-step generation, which consistently yielded the best results. We evaluated performance using both pixel-level and perceptual metrics. Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Structural Similarity Index Measure (SSIM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> were used to assess reconstruction fidelity. To measure perceptual quality and alignment with human visual perception, we employed Kernel Inception Distance (KID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, multiplied by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Performance comparison of Baseline and Inverse-aDDIM across four inverse tasks on the LSUN Bedroom dataset.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
